== ARCHIVE: Step M - water-dimer CCSD(T) ladder, counterpoise-corrected, official S22 geometry ==

Why this exists. paper/main.md section 4 carried an open item: the reported
counterpoise-corrected water-dimer interaction energy (-6.129 kcal/mol, omegaB97X-V/6-31g*,
grid level 2, from evidence/l2cp/20260928T213839Z_centos-step_h_l2cp.out:6) could not be
called right or wrong, because nothing separated "the functional binds too much" from
"our implementation is off". The sentence asked for a CCSD(T)/CBS reference from the
literature. It does not need one. What settles it is the same computation done at the same
geometry, the same basis and the same counterpoise convention with explicit electron
correlation instead of a density functional - which is what ran here, on this repository,
with no external number in the loop.

Run: OMP_NUM_THREADS=16 MAXMEM_MB=12000 BASES="6-31g*,cc-pvdz,aug-cc-pvdz,cc-pvtz,aug-cc-pvtz"
     PYTHONPATH=. python3 -u nlcsplit/step_m_ccsdt.py
     (log closed 2026-09-30T21:23Z, EXIT=0; the slowest single rung is aug-cc-pVTZ
     all-electron CCSD at 165 s and its CCSD(T) at 91 s; PySCF 2.12.1, Python 3.10.12, WSL)

Conventions, stated before any number is read out of this file:
  * geometry: official S22 entry 2 (scratch/lit/s22/S22_02.xyz), O...O = 2.9104 A, the
    initial (unrelaxed) complex geometry - the same segment the DFT row used. On a relaxed
    monomer geometry a correlated method binds deeper; that is baseline definition, not drift.
  * counterpoise only: E_dimer(full basis) - sum of the two half-molecules each computed with
    its own basis at the dimer geometry. The uncorrected column is NOT in this file, because
    it needs ghost functions and it is not what section 4 asks.
  * RHF reference; "frozen" correlates 8 of 10 occupied orbitals (two O 1s out, removed as
    frozen=2 on the dimer and frozen=1 on each monomer so the count cancels); "all-electron"
    correlates all 10. The two rungs are the measurement of what the frozen-core choice is
    worth at each basis.
  * MP2/CCSD/CCSD(T) come from PySCF's own modules, not from anything in nlcsplit.

A trap this run nearly fell into, recorded because it is the kind that survives review.
The first version froze the monomers unconditionally while switching only the dimer's
convention, so the "all-electron" rung compared an all-electron dimer against frozen-core
monomers. On 6-31g* it printed -8.67, and on cc-pVTZ -23.16 kcal/mol for the water dimer -
physically impossible, and the impossibility was entirely the 17 kcal of O 1s correlation
counted on one side of the difference only. After the fix the two rungs differ by 0.03 kcal
at 6-31g* and 0.50 at aug-cc-pVTZ, which is what core correlation is actually worth in a
double-zeta-quality basis.

